      "interpretation": "Expanded targeted decomposition still favors query-aware multi-span retrieval over full-note normalization and one-span baselines. In the 600-item hard slice from the lowest-performing held-out records, query-aware support reaches 395/600, with 238 auto-accepted supports, 157 review-required supports, 205 abstentions, and 0 high-risk supported items. Strict low-overlap review rescue adds only 3 supported items in this expanded run, all review-required. The full failed-exact pool then scales the same policy to 13,038 items: query-aware support reaches 11,001/13,038, with 7,829 auto-accepted supports, 3,172 review-required supports, 2,037 abstentions, and 0 high-risk supported items after converting 332 diffuse label-only unions to abstentions. The remaining failures show that the result is parsing-policy failure analysis, not semantic factuality, population prevalence, or proof that note length causally drives errors."
